<title>USA: Oregon's tax Measure 47 appears headed for passage.</title>
<headline>Oregon's tax Measure 47 appears headed for passage.</headline>
<dateline>PORTLAND, Ore. 1996-11-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>A controversial Oregon tax measure that would limit property taxes appeared headed for passage, according to analysts and local media Friday.</p>
<p>Although the vote count from Tuesday's election was still incomplete, Measure 47 took a narrow lead and is now expected to pass, they said.</p>
<p>Analyst Tim Hibbitts, an independent Portland pollster, said absentee voting trends made it certain the measure would pass. &quot;It's going to pass,&quot; he predicted.  </p>
<p>By cutting property taxes and capping their increase, Measure 47 would reduce local government financial flexibility, Moody's Investors Service Inc has warned.</p>
<p>A 20 percent reduction in revenue for both Oregon schools and local governments could result from the measure, according to recent studies.</p>
</text>
